At xFusionCorp Industries, the Stratos Datacenter houses a jump host server that stores template XML files essential for the Nautilus application. 
Prior to their use, these files need to be populated with valid data. 
As part of regular maintenance, the system administration team utilizes various string and file manipulation commands to prepare these templates.

Your task is to substitute all occurrences of the string String with Echo-Location within the XML file located at /root/nautilus.xml on the jump host server.

SOLUTION
Steps to Complete the Task
1. SSH into the Jump Host Server
bash
ssh username@jump-host-server
# Or use the specific hostname/IP provided in your environment
2. Switch to root (if needed)
Since the file is at /root/nautilus.xml, you'll likely need root or sudo access:

bash
sudo su -
# Or prefix commands with sudo
3. Verify the file exists and check current occurrences
bash
ls -l /root/nautilus.xml
grep -o "String" /root/nautilus.xml | wc -l
This will show how many times String currently appears in the file.

4. Substitute all occurrences of String with Echo-Location
Use sed with the global (g) flag to replace all occurrences on every line:

bash
sudo sed -i 's/String/Echo-Location/g' /root/nautilus.xml
Explanation of flags:

-i → Edit the file in-place (modifies the original file directly)

s/String/Echo-Location/g → Substitute String with Echo-Location globally (all occurrences per line)

/root/nautilus.xml → Target file

💡 Tip: If you want to create a backup before editing, use:

bash
sudo sed -i.bak 's/String/Echo-Location/g' /root/nautilus.xml
This creates nautilus.xml.bak as a backup.

5. Verify the substitution
bash
# Confirm no more "String" occurrences remain
grep -c "String" /root/nautilus.xml

# Confirm "Echo-Location" now appears
grep -o "Echo-Location" /root/nautilus.xml | wc -l

# View the modified file
cat /root/nautilus.xml
The grep -c "String" should return 0 (or nothing), and the count of Echo-Location should match the original count of String.

✅ One-Liner Solution
bash
sudo sed -i 's/String/Echo-Location/g' /root/nautilus.xml
📝 Alternative Approaches
Using perl
bash
sudo perl -pi -e 's/String/Echo-Location/g' /root/nautilus.xml
Using awk (writes to a new file, then replaces)
bash
sudo awk '{gsub(/String/,"Echo-Location")}1' /root/nautilus.xml > /tmp/nautilus.xml && sudo mv /tmp/nautilus.xml /root/nautilus.xml
Case-insensitive replacement (if needed)
bash
sudo sed -i 's/String/Echo-Location/gi' /root/nautilus.xml
⚠️ Important Considerations
Special characters in the replacement
Echo-Location contains a hyphen (-), which is safe in sed replacements. However, if your replacement string contained /, &, \, or other regex-special characters, you'd need to escape them or use a different delimiter:

bash
# Using a different delimiter (|) to avoid escaping slashes
sudo sed -i 's|String|Echo-Location|g' /root/nautilus.xml
Word boundary matching (optional)
If you only want to replace the standalone word String (not substrings like StringBuffer), use word boundaries:

bash
sudo sed -i 's/\bString\b/Echo-Location/g' /root/nautilus.xml
Based on the task wording ("all occurrences of the string String"), the straightforward global substitution without word boundaries is the expected solution.

🔍 Final Verification Checklist
bash
# 1. Confirm ownership/permissions unchanged
ls -l /root/nautilus.xml

# 2. Confirm no "String" remains
grep -n "String" /root/nautilus.xml

# 3. Confirm "Echo-Location" is present
grep -n "Echo-Location" /root/nautilus.xml

# 4. Display the final file
cat /root/nautilus.xml
The task is complete once all instances of String have been replaced with Echo-Location in /root/nautilus.xml.
